A1. The pedestrian who can do this has the jump on those who can't. ~ LEAP
A4. What cake-eaters like about cake. ~ ICING
A7. What an auto-mechanic gets his head into. ~ HOOD
A10. It isn't this that bothers Rastus—it's the spirit of the thing. (This is a grave matter.) ~ MOAN
A12. A Mohammedan prince. ~ EMIR
A14. Any car owner is bothered when he hears this in the back seat. ~ CHATTER
A17. What you might call a person who utters sharp, angry words. ~ SNAPPER
A19. The doughboy's burden. ~ KIT
A20. Anyone working with one of these—has to put up with him. ~ ERECTOR
A22. This covers many blocks in Washington. ~ HAT
A23. This supports a Follies girl. ~ LEG
A25. The Pope's triple crown. ~ TIARA
A26. The kind of attack Senator Heflin launches. ~ GAS
A27. What a jazz band gives you. ~ BLUES
A29. A ruminant quadruped inhabiting South Africa. ~ GNU
A30. These are often on their last legs. ~ PANTS
A32. What kind of candles did Aurelius write by? ~ ROMAN
A34. What do members of the Alimony Club refuse to abide by? ~ TERMS
A36. Jumbled type. ~ PI
A37. The skin the early Dutch traders loved to touch. ~ RED
A38. Use this—and with a certain amount of pull you should be able to get in anywhere. ~ NOB
A39. What is the correlative of either? ~ OR
A40. An ice-house. ~ IGLOO
A42. What do you call persons or things which are loved to excess? ~ IDOLS
A44. Who keeps the home-fires burning in the underworld? ~ DEVIL
A46. This will give you a dirty dig—if you get the point ~ PIN
A48. This runs around a lot. ~ FENCE
A50. Give another name for a choker. ~ BOA
A51. These are poisonous. ~ FANGS
A53. Where the little fish come from. ~ ROE
A54. A preposition. ~ FOR
A55. What would you call a strike at the Government red-tape factory. ~ WALKOUI
A57. A short song or lyric poem. ~ ODE
A59. When these are wild any good citizen would like to see them put behind the bars. ~ ANIMALS
A61. Many a bridge fiend has done this to his opponent. ~ TRUMPED
A63. Between what are vacuums found? (Particularly applicable'to a certain Senator from Alabama.) ~ EARS
A65. Who plucked an Irish rose? ~ ABIE
A66. A word expressing obligation or necessity. ~ MUST
A67. This word means to kick out by authority. ~ EXPEL
A68. This is often well-loaded. ~ DRAY

D1. A lady often appealed to by gentlemen of African descent. ~ LUCK
D2. What kind of athlete would "die for dear old Rutgers?" ~ AMATEUR
D3. Poker players strive for this. ~ POT
D4. This word means lifeless; sluggish. ~ INERT
D5. A conjunction meaning a condition or supposition. ~ IF
D6. A famous Italian seaport. ~ GENOA
D7. A liquor carrier. ~ HIP
D8. What will Judge, Jr., and Judgette be when His Honor goes on to a Higher Court? ~ ORPHANS
D9. The prey of Old Dutch Cleanser. ~ DIRT
D11. What did the three bears do to the porridge? ~ ATE
D13. To disfigure. ~ MAR
D15. What would you like to do on high? ~ HILL
D16. The Prince of Wales will be able to do this some day. ~ REIGN
D17. What the old time cake-walkers have learned to do. ~ STRUT
D18. What did Columbus hope to find when he went west? ~ EAST
D21. What do they put Lizzie Labels on? ~ CAN
D24. A Southern State. ~ GEORGIA
D26. This chap might be called a risky fellow. ~ GAMBLER
D27. This has two feet on the ground. ~ BIPED
D28. What the little fishes do at market. ~ SMELL
D30. A fisherman rarely offers this when he tella of his catch. ~ PROOF
D31. A frolic with Judge, Jr., and Mac. ~ SPREE
D33. Bustle. ~ ADO
D35. What does everything come to sooner or later? ~ END
D40. What makes music when tickled? ~ IVORIES
D41. Precious stones. ~ OPALS
D42. A mass of metal cast in a mold. ~ INGOT
D43. A government agent. ~ SNOOPER
D45. This is dark stuff. ~ EBON
D47. Say it with flowers—but never with this. ~ INK
D49. To surrender. ~ CEDE
D51. What many promising young men become. ~ FALSE
D52. This word pertains to the calf of the leg. ~ SURAL
D54. Where should people know their onions? ~ FARM
D55. There's a lot of big guns in this. ~ WAR
D56. This holds a lot of suds. ~ IUB
D58. This goes around in wet places. ~ EDDY
D60. This is always under foot. ~ MAT
D62. This section is never on edge. ~ MID
D64. Where is the over head? ~ UP


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
D30: A fisherman rarely offers this when he tella of his catch. → tells

